186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557201 #edb/ref 1077 "field-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557201 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557201 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f267bf00-331a-5ba4-80d1-f50c27b73d21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-3x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=5y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "257d204a-c14e-5aa5-8045-bec1e0148274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557220 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5xy=0 \\\\ 5y-3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+5xy & =0 \\\\ x(-3+5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-3xy & =0 \\\\ y(5-3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(5-3x) & =0 \\\\ 5-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{3}{5}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79cf6220-8640-5993-92c2-8195fa289578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557273 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0398fe30-8e44-5ba6-8fd1-a43e6091f841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557338 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557338 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0bd04562-8813-5c77-9599-36a65de31f18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557338 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186557273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557373 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186557338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557373 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "840e4163-cfbd-5a7e-8f5f-200b5e5bc9bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557373 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186557220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557407 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5c129fa-a782-5dfd-8c7f-1026592e7785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557407 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186370293 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
